/-!
# Noether's theorem for Lagrangian systems

Let `E` be a real normed space, the configuration space of a mechanical system, and let
`L : ℝ → E × E → ℝ` be a possibly time-dependent Lagrangian, a function `L t (q, v)` of time,
positions `q` and velocities `v`. A curve `q` with velocity `v` solves the **Euler–Lagrange
equations** when `d/dt ∂_v L(t, q, v) = ∂_q L(t, q, v)`.

An *infinitesimal symmetry* of `L` is a possibly time-dependent vector field `ξ(t, q)` on the
configuration space. It moves a point `(q, v)` of the velocity phase space along its first
prolongation `(ξ(t, q), ∂_t ξ(t, q) + D_q ξ(t, q) v)`; the second component is
`Dξ_(t, q)(1, v)`, the derivative of `ξ` in the direction of the velocity `(1, v)` of the curve
`t ↦ (t, q(t))`. The derivative of `L` along this prolongation is the **infinitesimal variation**

`δ_ξ L(t, q, v) = ∂_q L(t, q, v)(ξ(t, q)) + ∂_v L(t, q, v)(Dξ_(t, q)(1, v))`

(`TauCeti.lagrangianVariation`). The vector field `ξ` is a symmetry up to a *gauge term*
`B(t, q)` when this variation is the total time derivative `DB_(t, q)(1, v)` of `B`, that is,
`δ_ξ L = dB/dt` along curves. **Noether's theorem** then says that the **Noether charge**

`J(t, q, v) = ∂_v L(t, q, v)(ξ(t, q)) - B(t, q)`

(`TauCeti.noetherCharge`) is conserved along every solution of the Euler–Lagrange equations
(`TauCeti.hasDerivAt_noetherCharge`, `TauCeti.noetherCharge_eq_of_eulerLagrange`). The symmetry
condition is only used along the solution, and only at the times considered.

Two classical sources of symmetries with `B = 0` are invariance of `L` under translation in a
direction `e`, which makes the momentum `∂_v L(e)` conserved
(`TauCeti.lagrangianVariation_const_eq_zero`), and invariance under a one-parameter family of
linear maps `g s` with `g 0 = 1` and generator `A = g'(0)`
(`TauCeti.lagrangianVariation_comp_snd_eq_zero`), such as the rotations generated by a
skew-adjoint `A`. For a linear generator `ξ(t, q) = A q` and `B = 0`, the Noether charge is the
cotangent-lift moment map `⟨μ(q, p), A⟩ = p(A q)` of the linear action
(`TauCeti.cotangentMomentMap`) evaluated at the Legendre image `(q, ∂_v L(t, q, v))`
(`TauCeti.noetherCharge_eq_cotangentMomentMap`). The Galilean boosts `ξ(t, q) = t e` of a
particle `L = (m / 2) ‖v‖² - U(q)` in a potential invariant in the direction `e` need the gauge
term `B(t, q) = m ⟪q, e⟫`. Their charge `m (t ⟪v, e⟫ - ⟪q, e⟫)` is conserved, which says that
the `e`-component of the motion is uniform (`TauCeti.mul_inner_sub_inner_eq_of_eulerLagrange`).

The charge, the variation and the symmetry condition are independent of the configuration
coordinates. Let `φ : E → F` be a change of coordinates and `L'` a Lagrangian on `F` with
`L(t, q, v) = L'(t, φ(q), Dφ_q v)`, and let `ξ'` be `φ`-related to `ξ`, that is,
`ξ'(t, φ(q)) = Dφ_q ξ(t, q)`. Then the charges agree at `(q, v)` and its image
`(φ(q), Dφ_q v)` when the gauge terms do (`TauCeti.noetherCharge_eq_of_comp`). For `C²`
coordinate changes, the variations agree as well (`TauCeti.lagrangianVariation_eq_of_comp`), as do
the total time derivatives of gauge terms related by `B(t, q) = B'(t, φ(q))`, so the symmetry
condition holds in one set of coordinates exactly when it holds in the other
(`TauCeti.lagrangianVariation_eq_fderiv_iff_of_comp`). No invertibility of `φ` is needed.

All statements are pointwise in time and in the velocity phase space, so they apply equally to
local solutions and to local coordinate changes.

## Main definitions

* `TauCeti.noetherCharge L ξ B`: the Noether charge `∂_v L(t, q, v)(ξ(t, q)) - B(t, q)`.
* `TauCeti.lagrangianVariation L ξ`: the infinitesimal variation of `L` along the first
  prolongation of `ξ`.

## Main results

* `TauCeti.hasDerivAt_noetherCharge`, `TauCeti.noetherCharge_eq_of_eulerLagrange`: Noether's
  theorem.
* `TauCeti.noetherCharge_eq_cotangentMomentMap`: comparison with the cotangent-lift moment map.
* `TauCeti.noetherCharge_eq_of_comp`, `TauCeti.lagrangianVariation_eq_of_comp`,
  `TauCeti.lagrangianVariation_eq_fderiv_iff_of_comp`: the transformation law under changes of
  configuration coordinates.

## References

* E. Noether, *Invariante Variationsprobleme*, Nachrichten von der Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften
  zu Göttingen, Mathematisch-Physikalische Klasse (1918), 235–257.
* V. I. Arnold, *Mathematical Methods of Classical Mechanics*, 2nd ed., Graduate Texts in
  Mathematics 60, Springer, 1989, Section 20.
* J. E. Marsden, T. S. Ratiu, *Introduction to Mechanics and Symmetry*, 2nd ed., Texts in Applied
  Mathematics 17, Springer, 1999, Sections 7.3 and 11.4.
* P. J. Olver, *Applications of Lie Groups to Differential Equations*, 2nd ed., Graduate Texts in
  Mathematics 107, Springer, 1993, Section 4.4 (divergence symmetries).
-/

public section

namespace TauCeti

open ContinuousLinearMap Filter Topology

variable {E F : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E] [NormedAddCommGroup F]
  [NormedSpace ℝ F]

/-! ### The Noether charge and the infinitesimal variation -/

/-- The **Noether charge** of a time-dependent Lagrangian `L`, an infinitesimal symmetry `ξ` and a
gauge term `B`: at time `t` and at `(q, v)`, the momentum `∂_v L(t, q, v)` paired with `ξ(t, q)`,
minus `B(t, q)`. -/
noncomputable def noetherCharge (L : ℝ → E × E → ℝ) (ξ : ℝ × E → E) (B : ℝ × E → ℝ) (t : ℝ)
    (x : E × E) : ℝ :=
  fderiv ℝ (fun u ↦ L t (x.1, u)) x.2 (ξ (t, x.1)) - B (t, x.1)

theorem noetherCharge_apply (L : ℝ → E × E → ℝ) (ξ : ℝ × E → E) (B : ℝ × E → ℝ) (t : ℝ)
    (x : E × E) :
    noetherCharge L ξ B t x = fderiv ℝ (fun u ↦ L t (x.1, u)) x.2 (ξ (t, x.1)) - B (t, x.1) :=
  (rfl)

/-- The **infinitesimal variation** of a time-dependent Lagrangian `L` under a time-dependent vector
field `ξ`: at time `t` and at `(q, v)`, the derivative of `L t` in the direction of the first
prolongation `(ξ(t, q), Dξ_(t, q)(1, v))` of `ξ`, where
`Dξ_(t, q)(1, v) = ∂_t ξ(t, q) + D_q ξ(t, q) v`. -/
noncomputable def lagrangianVariation (L : ℝ → E × E → ℝ) (ξ : ℝ × E → E) (t : ℝ) (x : E × E) :
    ℝ :=
  fderiv ℝ (L t) x (ξ (t, x.1), fderiv ℝ ξ (t, x.1) (1, x.2))

theorem lagrangianVariation_apply (L : ℝ → E × E → ℝ) (ξ : ℝ × E → E) (t : ℝ) (x : E × E) :
    lagrangianVariation L ξ t x = fderiv ℝ (L t) x (ξ (t, x.1), fderiv ℝ ξ (t, x.1) (1, x.2)) :=
  (rfl)

/-- Where `L t` is differentiable, the infinitesimal variation is
`∂_q L(t, q, v)(ξ(t, q)) + ∂_v L(t, q, v)(Dξ_(t, q)(1, v))`. -/
theorem lagrangianVariation_eq_add {L : ℝ → E × E → ℝ} {t : ℝ} {x : E × E}
    (hL : DifferentiableAt ℝ (L t) x) (ξ : ℝ × E → E) :
    lagrangianVariation L ξ t x =
      fderiv ℝ (fun y ↦ L t (y, x.2)) x.1 (ξ (t, x.1)) +
        fderiv ℝ (fun u ↦ L t (x.1, u)) x.2 (fderiv ℝ ξ (t, x.1) (1, x.2)) := by
  have hL' : DifferentiableAt ℝ (L t) (x.1, x.2) := hL
  rw [lagrangianVariation_apply, fderiv_slice_left hL', fderiv_slice_right hL']
  simp only [coe_comp, Function.comp_apply, inl_apply, inr_apply, ← map_add, Prod.mk_add_mk,
    add_zero, zero_add]

/-! ### Noether's theorem -/

/-- **Noether's theorem**, pointwise in time. Let `q` have velocity `v t` at `t` and satisfy the
Euler–Lagrange equation `d/dt ∂_v L(t, q, v) = ∂_q L(t, q, v)` there. If at `t` the infinitesimal
variation of `L` under `ξ` is the total time derivative `DB_(t, q)(1, v)` of the gauge term `B`
along the curve, then the Noether charge has derivative zero at `t`. -/
theorem hasDerivAt_noetherCharge {L : ℝ → E × E → ℝ} {ξ : ℝ × E → E} {B : ℝ × E → ℝ}
    {q v : ℝ → E} {t : ℝ} (hq : HasDerivAt q (v t) t)
    (hEL : HasDerivAt (fun s ↦ fderiv ℝ (fun u ↦ L s (q s, u)) (v s))
      (fderiv ℝ (fun y ↦ L t (y, v t)) (q t)) t)
    (hL : DifferentiableAt ℝ (L t) (q t, v t)) (hξ : DifferentiableAt ℝ ξ (t, q t))
    (hB : DifferentiableAt ℝ B (t, q t))
    (hsym : lagrangianVariation L ξ t (q t, v t) = fderiv ℝ B (t, q t) (1, v t)) :
    HasDerivAt (fun s ↦ noetherCharge L ξ B s (q s, v s)) 0 t := by
  -- The curve `s ↦ (s, q s)` in extended configuration space has velocity `(1, v t)`.
  have hγ : HasDerivAt (fun s ↦ (s, q s)) (1, v t) t := (hasDerivAt_id t).prodMk hq
  have hξc : HasDerivAt (fun s ↦ ξ (s, q s)) (fderiv ℝ ξ (t, q t) (1, v t)) t :=
    hξ.hasFDerivAt.comp_hasDerivAt t hγ
  have hBc : HasDerivAt (fun s ↦ B (s, q s)) (fderiv ℝ B (t, q t) (1, v t)) t :=
    hB.hasFDerivAt.comp_hasDerivAt t hγ
  -- By the product rule and the Euler–Lagrange equation, the momentum term has derivative the
  -- infinitesimal variation, and the symmetry condition cancels it against the gauge term.
  refine ((hEL.clm_apply hξc).sub hBc).congr_deriv ?_
  rw [← hsym, lagrangianVariation_eq_add hL]
  exact sub_self _

/-- **Noether's theorem.** Let `q` solve the Euler–Lagrange equations with velocity `v` on an open
preconnected set of times `s`, and suppose that, at every time of `s`, the infinitesimal variation
of `L` under `ξ` along the solution is the total time derivative of the gauge term `B`. Then the
Noether charge takes the same value at all times of `s`. -/
theorem noetherCharge_eq_of_eulerLagrange {L : ℝ → E × E → ℝ} {ξ : ℝ × E → E} {B : ℝ × E → ℝ}
    {q v : ℝ → E} {s : Set ℝ} (hs : IsOpen s) (hs' : IsPreconnected s)
    (hq : ∀ t ∈ s, HasDerivAt q (v t) t)
    (hEL : ∀ t ∈ s, HasDerivAt (fun s ↦ fderiv ℝ (fun u ↦ L s (q s, u)) (v s))
      (fderiv ℝ (fun y ↦ L t (y, v t)) (q t)) t)
    (hL : ∀ t ∈ s, DifferentiableAt ℝ (L t) (q t, v t))
    (hξ : ∀ t ∈ s, DifferentiableAt ℝ ξ (t, q t)) (hB : ∀ t ∈ s, DifferentiableAt ℝ B (t, q t))
    (hsym : ∀ t ∈ s, lagrangianVariation L ξ t (q t, v t) = fderiv ℝ B (t, q t) (1, v t))
    {t₀ t₁ : ℝ} (h₀ : t₀ ∈ s) (h₁ : t₁ ∈ s) :
    noetherCharge L ξ B t₀ (q t₀, v t₀) = noetherCharge L ξ B t₁ (q t₁, v t₁) := by
  have hderiv (t : ℝ) (ht : t ∈ s) :
      HasDerivAt (fun s ↦ noetherCharge L ξ B s (q s, v s)) 0 t :=
    hasDerivAt_noetherCharge (hq t ht) (hEL t ht) (hL t ht) (hξ t ht) (hB t ht) (hsym t ht)
  exact hs.is_const_of_deriv_eq_zero hs'
    (fun t ht ↦ (hderiv t ht).differentiableAt.differentiableWithinAt)
    (fun t ht ↦ (hderiv t ht).deriv) h₀ h₁

/-! ### Symmetries from invariance of the Lagrangian -/

/-- For a constant vector field `e`, the infinitesimal variation is the derivative `∂_q L(e)` of
`L` in the position direction `e`. -/
@[simp]
theorem lagrangianVariation_const (L : ℝ → E × E → ℝ) (e : E) (t : ℝ) (x : E × E) :
    lagrangianVariation L (fun _ ↦ e) t x = fderiv ℝ (L t) x (e, 0) := by
  simp [lagrangianVariation_apply]

/-- **Translation invariance.** If `L t` is differentiable at `x` and invariant under translation
of the position in the direction `e` near `x`, then the constant vector field `e` is an
infinitesimal symmetry of `L` at `t` and `x`, with zero variation. Its Noether charge with zero
gauge term is the momentum `∂_v L(t, q, v)(e)`. -/
theorem lagrangianVariation_const_eq_zero {L : ℝ → E × E → ℝ} {e : E} {t : ℝ} {x : E × E}
    (hL : DifferentiableAt ℝ (L t) x) (hinv : ∀ᶠ s in 𝓝 (0 : ℝ), L t (x.1 + s • e, x.2) = L t x) :
    lagrangianVariation L (fun _ ↦ e) t x = 0 := by
  have hc : HasDerivAt (fun s : ℝ ↦ (x.1 + s • e, x.2)) (e, 0) 0 :=
    (HasDerivAt.prodMk (((hasDerivAt_id (0 : ℝ)).smul_const e).const_add x.1)
      (hasDerivAt_const _ _)).congr_deriv (by simp)
  have hd : HasDerivAt (fun s : ℝ ↦ L t (x.1 + s • e, x.2)) (fderiv ℝ (L t) x (e, 0)) 0 :=
    hL.hasFDerivAt.comp_hasDerivAt_of_eq (0 : ℝ) hc (by simp)
  rw [lagrangianVariation_const]
  exact hd.unique ((hasDerivAt_const (0 : ℝ) (L t x)).congr_of_eventuallyEq hinv)

/-- For a linear vector field `ξ(t, q) = A q`, the infinitesimal variation is the derivative of `L`
in the direction `(A q, A v)` of the tangent lift of `A`. -/
@[simp]
theorem lagrangianVariation_comp_snd (L : ℝ → E × E → ℝ) (A : E →L[ℝ] E) (t : ℝ) (x : E × E) :
    lagrangianVariation L (A ∘ Prod.snd) t x = fderiv ℝ (L t) x (A x.1, A x.2) := by
  have hA : HasFDerivAt (A ∘ Prod.snd : ℝ × E → E) (A.comp (snd ℝ ℝ E)) (t, x.1) :=
    A.hasFDerivAt.comp (t, x.1) (hasFDerivAt_snd)
  simp [lagrangianVariation_apply, hA.fderiv]

/-- **Invariance under a linear family.** Let `g s` be continuous linear maps of `E` with `g 0 = 1`
and derivative `A` at `0`. If `L t` is differentiable at `x` and `L t (g s q, g s v) = L t (q, v)`
for `s` near `0`, then the linear vector field `A` is an infinitesimal symmetry of `L` at `t` and
`x`, with zero variation. For the rotations `g s = exp(s A)` generated by a skew-adjoint `A`, the
Noether charge is the angular momentum `∂_v L(A q)`. -/
theorem lagrangianVariation_comp_snd_eq_zero {L : ℝ → E × E → ℝ} {g : ℝ → E →L[ℝ] E}
    {A : E →L[ℝ] E} {t : ℝ} {x : E × E} (hg₀ : g 0 = 1) (hg : HasDerivAt g A 0)
    (hL : DifferentiableAt ℝ (L t) x)
    (hinv : ∀ᶠ s in 𝓝 (0 : ℝ), L t (g s x.1, g s x.2) = L t x) :
    lagrangianVariation L (A ∘ Prod.snd) t x = 0 := by
  have hc : HasDerivAt (fun s : ℝ ↦ (g s x.1, g s x.2)) (A x.1, A x.2) 0 :=
    (HasDerivAt.prodMk (hg.clm_apply (hasDerivAt_const _ x.1))
      (hg.clm_apply (hasDerivAt_const _ x.2))).congr_deriv (by simp)
  have hd : HasDerivAt (fun s : ℝ ↦ L t (g s x.1, g s x.2)) (fderiv ℝ (L t) x (A x.1, A x.2)) 0 :=
    hL.hasFDerivAt.comp_hasDerivAt_of_eq (0 : ℝ) hc (by simp [hg₀])
  rw [lagrangianVariation_comp_snd]
  exact hd.unique ((hasDerivAt_const (0 : ℝ) (L t x)).congr_of_eventuallyEq hinv)

/-- **The Noether charge of a linear symmetry is the cotangent-lift moment map.** For a linear
vector field `ξ(t, q) = A q` and zero gauge term, the Noether charge at `(q, v)` is the moment map
`⟨μ(q, p), A⟩ = p(A q)` of the cotangent-lifted linear action, at the momentum
`p = ∂_v L(t, q, v)`. -/
theorem noetherCharge_eq_cotangentMomentMap (L : ℝ → E × E → ℝ) (A : E →L[ℝ] E) (t : ℝ)
    (x : E × E) :
    noetherCharge L (A ∘ Prod.snd) 0 t x =
      cotangentMomentMap (x.1, fderiv ℝ (fun u ↦ L t (x.1, u)) x.2) (A : E →ₗ[ℝ] E) := by
  simp [noetherCharge_apply]

/-! ### Changes of configuration coordinates -/

/-- **The Noether charge is independent of coordinates.** Let `φ : E → F` be a map of
configuration spaces, and let `L'` be a Lagrangian on `F` with `L(t, q, u) = L'(t, φ q, Dφ_q u)`
for velocities `u` near `v`, where the velocity map `u ↦ L'(t, φ q, u)` is differentiable at
`Dφ_q v`. If `ξ'(t, φ q) = Dφ_q ξ(t, q)` and `B(t, q) = B'(t, φ q)`, then the Noether charges agree
at `(q, v)` and at `(φ q, Dφ_q v)`. -/
theorem noetherCharge_eq_of_comp {L : ℝ → E × E → ℝ} {L' : ℝ → F × F → ℝ} {φ : E → F}
    {ξ : ℝ × E → E} {ξ' : ℝ × F → F} {B : ℝ × E → ℝ} {B' : ℝ × F → ℝ} {t : ℝ} {x : E × E}
    (hL : (fun u ↦ L t (x.1, u)) =ᶠ[𝓝 x.2] fun u ↦ L' t (φ x.1, fderiv ℝ φ x.1 u))
    (hL' : DifferentiableAt ℝ (fun u ↦ L' t (φ x.1, u)) (fderiv ℝ φ x.1 x.2))
    (hξ : ξ' (t, φ x.1) = fderiv ℝ φ x.1 (ξ (t, x.1))) (hB : B (t, x.1) = B' (t, φ x.1)) :
    noetherCharge L ξ B t x = noetherCharge L' ξ' B' t (φ x.1, fderiv ℝ φ x.1 x.2) := by
  have h : fderiv ℝ (fun u ↦ L t (x.1, u)) x.2 =
      (fderiv ℝ (fun u ↦ L' t (φ x.1, u)) (fderiv ℝ φ x.1 x.2)).comp (fderiv ℝ φ x.1) := by
    rw [hL.fderiv_eq]
    exact (hL'.hasFDerivAt.comp x.2 (fderiv ℝ φ x.1).hasFDerivAt).fderiv
  rw [noetherCharge_apply, noetherCharge_apply, h, ContinuousLinearMap.comp_apply, hξ, hB]

/-- **The infinitesimal variation is independent of coordinates.** Let `φ : E → F` be `C²` at `q`,
and let `L'` be a Lagrangian on `F` with `L(t, y, u) = L'(t, φ y, Dφ_y u)` near `(q, v)`, with
`L' t` differentiable at `(φ q, Dφ_q v)`. If `ξ'(s, φ y) = Dφ_y ξ(s, y)` near `(t, q)`, with `ξ`
and `ξ'` differentiable at `(t, q)` and `(t, φ q)`, then the infinitesimal variations agree at
`(q, v)` and at `(φ q, Dφ_q v)`. -/
theorem lagrangianVariation_eq_of_comp {L : ℝ → E × E → ℝ} {L' : ℝ → F × F → ℝ} {φ : E → F}
    {ξ : ℝ × E → E} {ξ' : ℝ × F → F} {t : ℝ} {x : E × E}
    (hL : L t =ᶠ[𝓝 x] fun y ↦ L' t (φ y.1, fderiv ℝ φ y.1 y.2))
    (hL' : DifferentiableAt ℝ (L' t) (φ x.1, fderiv ℝ φ x.1 x.2)) (hφ : ContDiffAt ℝ 2 φ x.1)
    (hξ : (fun z : ℝ × E ↦ ξ' (z.1, φ z.2)) =ᶠ[𝓝 (t, x.1)] fun z ↦ fderiv ℝ φ z.2 (ξ z))
    (hξd : DifferentiableAt ℝ ξ (t, x.1)) (hξ'd : DifferentiableAt ℝ ξ' (t, φ x.1)) :
    lagrangianVariation L ξ t x = lagrangianVariation L' ξ' t (φ x.1, fderiv ℝ φ x.1 x.2) := by
  have hφd : DifferentiableAt ℝ φ x.1 := hφ.differentiableAt (by norm_num)
  have hDφ : DifferentiableAt ℝ (fderiv ℝ φ) x.1 :=
    (hφ.fderiv_right (m := 1) (by norm_num)).differentiableAt (by norm_num)
  have hsymm : IsSymmSndFDerivAt ℝ φ x.1 := hφ.isSymmSndFDerivAt (by simp)
  set D := fderiv ℝ φ x.1
  set D₂ := fderiv ℝ (fderiv ℝ φ) x.1
  -- The tangent map `(y, u) ↦ (φ y, Dφ_y u)` has derivative `(a, b) ↦ (D a, D b + D₂ a u)`.
  have hT : HasFDerivAt (fun y : E × E ↦ (φ y.1, fderiv ℝ φ y.1 y.2))
      ((D.comp (fst ℝ E E)).prod (D.comp (snd ℝ E E) + (D₂.comp (fst ℝ E E)).flip x.2)) x :=
    (hφd.hasFDerivAt.comp x hasFDerivAt_fst).prodMk
      ((hDφ.hasFDerivAt.comp x hasFDerivAt_fst).clm_apply hasFDerivAt_snd)
  have hLx : fderiv ℝ (L t) x = (fderiv ℝ (L' t) (φ x.1, D x.2)).comp
      ((D.comp (fst ℝ E E)).prod (D.comp (snd ℝ E E) + (D₂.comp (fst ℝ E E)).flip x.2)) := by
    rw [hL.fderiv_eq]
    exact (hL'.hasFDerivAt.comp x hT).fderiv
  -- Differentiating the relation `ξ'(s, φ y) = Dφ_y ξ(s, y)` at `(t, q)` in the direction `(1, v)`
  -- relates the prolongations of `ξ` and `ξ'`.
  have h₁ : HasFDerivAt (fun z : ℝ × E ↦ ξ' (z.1, φ z.2))
      ((fderiv ℝ ξ' (t, φ x.1)).comp ((fst ℝ ℝ E).prod (D.comp (snd ℝ ℝ E)))) (t, x.1) :=
    hξ'd.hasFDerivAt.comp (t, x.1)
      (hasFDerivAt_fst.prodMk (hφd.hasFDerivAt.comp (t, x.1) hasFDerivAt_snd))
  have h₂ : HasFDerivAt (fun z : ℝ × E ↦ fderiv ℝ φ z.2 (ξ z))
      (D.comp (fderiv ℝ ξ (t, x.1)) + (D₂.comp (snd ℝ ℝ E)).flip (ξ (t, x.1))) (t, x.1) :=
    (hDφ.hasFDerivAt.comp (t, x.1) hasFDerivAt_snd).clm_apply hξd.hasFDerivAt
  have hrel := congrArg (fun f ↦ f (1, x.2)) (h₁.unique (h₂.congr_of_eventuallyEq hξ))
  simp only [coe_comp, Function.comp_apply, ContinuousLinearMap.prod_apply, coe_fst', coe_snd',
    add_apply, flip_apply] at hrel
  rw [lagrangianVariation_apply, lagrangianVariation_apply, hLx, hξ.eq_of_nhds]
  simp only [coe_comp, Function.comp_apply, ContinuousLinearMap.prod_apply, coe_fst', coe_snd',
    add_apply, flip_apply, hrel, hsymm (ξ (t, x.1)) x.2, D₂, D]

/-- **The symmetry condition is independent of coordinates.** Under the hypotheses of
`TauCeti.lagrangianVariation_eq_of_comp`, let the gauge terms be related by
`B(s, y) = B'(s, φ y)` near `(t, q)`, with `B'` differentiable at `(t, φ q)`. Then the infinitesimal
variation of `L` under `ξ` at `(q, v)` is the total time derivative of `B` exactly when the
infinitesimal variation of `L'` under `ξ'` at `(φ q, Dφ_q v)` is the total time derivative of
`B'`. -/
theorem lagrangianVariation_eq_fderiv_iff_of_comp {L : ℝ → E × E → ℝ} {L' : ℝ → F × F → ℝ}
    {φ : E → F} {ξ : ℝ × E → E} {ξ' : ℝ × F → F} {B : ℝ × E → ℝ} {B' : ℝ × F → ℝ} {t : ℝ}
    {x : E × E} (hL : L t =ᶠ[𝓝 x] fun y ↦ L' t (φ y.1, fderiv ℝ φ y.1 y.2))
    (hL' : DifferentiableAt ℝ (L' t) (φ x.1, fderiv ℝ φ x.1 x.2)) (hφ : ContDiffAt ℝ 2 φ x.1)
    (hξ : (fun z : ℝ × E ↦ ξ' (z.1, φ z.2)) =ᶠ[𝓝 (t, x.1)] fun z ↦ fderiv ℝ φ z.2 (ξ z))
    (hξd : DifferentiableAt ℝ ξ (t, x.1)) (hξ'd : DifferentiableAt ℝ ξ' (t, φ x.1))
    (hB : B =ᶠ[𝓝 (t, x.1)] fun z ↦ B' (z.1, φ z.2)) (hB' : DifferentiableAt ℝ B' (t, φ x.1)) :
    lagrangianVariation L ξ t x = fderiv ℝ B (t, x.1) (1, x.2) ↔
      lagrangianVariation L' ξ' t (φ x.1, fderiv ℝ φ x.1 x.2) =
        fderiv ℝ B' (t, φ x.1) (1, fderiv ℝ φ x.1 x.2) := by
  have hφd : DifferentiableAt ℝ φ x.1 := hφ.differentiableAt (by norm_num)
  have hBx : fderiv ℝ B (t, x.1) = (fderiv ℝ B' (t, φ x.1)).comp
      ((fst ℝ ℝ E).prod ((fderiv ℝ φ x.1).comp (snd ℝ ℝ E))) := by
    rw [hB.fderiv_eq]
    exact (hB'.hasFDerivAt.comp (t, x.1)
      (hasFDerivAt_fst.prodMk (hφd.hasFDerivAt.comp (t, x.1) hasFDerivAt_snd))).fderiv
  simp [lagrangianVariation_eq_of_comp hL hL' hφ hξ hξd hξ'd, hBx]

/-! ### Galilean boosts -/

open RealInnerProductSpace

variable {V : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup V] [InnerProductSpace ℝ V]

/-- **Galilean boosts.** Let a particle of nonzero mass `m` move in a potential `U`, with Lagrangian
`L(q, v) = (m / 2) ‖v‖² - U(q)`, along a solution of the Euler–Lagrange equations on an open
preconnected set of times. If along the solution `U` is differentiable with `DU(e) = 0`, as when
`U` is invariant under translation in the direction `e`, then the boost `ξ(t, q) = t e` is a
symmetry with gauge term `B(t, q) = m ⟪q, e⟫`. Its conserved Noether charge shows that
`t ⟪v t, e⟫ - ⟪q t, e⟫` is constant: the `e`-component of the position moves uniformly. -/
theorem mul_inner_sub_inner_eq_of_eulerLagrange {m : ℝ} (hm : m ≠ 0) {U : V → ℝ} {e : V}
    {q v : ℝ → V} {s : Set ℝ} (hs : IsOpen s) (hs' : IsPreconnected s)
    (hU : ∀ t ∈ s, DifferentiableAt ℝ U (q t)) (hUe : ∀ t ∈ s, fderiv ℝ U (q t) e = 0)
    (hq : ∀ t ∈ s, HasDerivAt q (v t) t)
    (hEL : ∀ t ∈ s, HasDerivAt (fun s ↦ fderiv ℝ (fun u ↦ m / 2 * ‖u‖ ^ 2 - U (q s)) (v s))
      (fderiv ℝ (fun y ↦ m / 2 * ‖v t‖ ^ 2 - U y) (q t)) t)
    {t₀ t₁ : ℝ} (h₀ : t₀ ∈ s) (h₁ : t₁ ∈ s) :
    t₀ * ⟪v t₀, e⟫ - ⟪q t₀, e⟫ = t₁ * ⟪v t₁, e⟫ - ⟪q t₁, e⟫ := by
  set L : ℝ → V × V → ℝ := fun _ x ↦ m / 2 * ‖x.2‖ ^ 2 - U x.1
  set ξ : ℝ × V → V := fun z ↦ z.1 • e
  set B : ℝ × V → ℝ := fun z ↦ m * ⟪z.2, e⟫
  -- The derivatives of the Lagrangian, of the boost and of the gauge term.
  have hLd (t : ℝ) {x : V × V} (hx : DifferentiableAt ℝ U x.1) : HasFDerivAt (L t)
      ((m / 2) • (2 • innerSL ℝ x.2).comp (snd ℝ V V) - (fderiv ℝ U x.1).comp (fst ℝ V V)) x :=
    ((hasFDerivAt_snd.norm_sq).const_mul (m / 2)).sub (hx.hasFDerivAt.comp x hasFDerivAt_fst)
  have hv (y u : V) : fderiv ℝ (fun w ↦ m / 2 * ‖w‖ ^ 2 - U y) u = (m / 2) • (2 • innerSL ℝ u) :=
    (((hasFDerivAt_id u).norm_sq).const_mul (m / 2)).sub_const (U y) |>.fderiv
  have hξd (z : ℝ × V) : HasFDerivAt ξ ((fst ℝ ℝ V).smulRight e) z :=
    (hasFDerivAt_fst.smul_const e)
  have hBd (z : ℝ × V) : HasFDerivAt B (m • (innerSL ℝ e).comp (snd ℝ ℝ V)) z := by
    have := ((innerSL ℝ e).hasFDerivAt.comp z (hasFDerivAt_snd (p := z))).const_mul m
    refine this.congr_of_eventuallyEq (Eventually.of_forall fun z' ↦ ?_)
    simp [B, real_inner_comm]
  have hcharge (t : ℝ) : noetherCharge L ξ B t (q t, v t) = m * (t * ⟪v t, e⟫ - ⟪q t, e⟫) := by
    rw [noetherCharge_apply]
    simp only [L, ξ, B]
    rw [hv]
    simp
    ring
  have key := noetherCharge_eq_of_eulerLagrange (L := L) (ξ := ξ) (B := B) hs hs' hq hEL
    (fun t ht ↦ (hLd t (hU t ht)).differentiableAt) (fun t _ ↦ (hξd _).differentiableAt)
    (fun t _ ↦ (hBd _).differentiableAt) (fun t ht ↦ by
      rw [lagrangianVariation_apply, (hLd t (hU t ht)).fderiv, (hξd _).fderiv, (hBd _).fderiv]
      simp [ξ, hUe t ht, real_inner_comm]
      ring) h₀ h₁
  rw [hcharge, hcharge] at key
  exact mul_left_cancel₀ hm key

end TauCeti
